NAME
    Maat - a spec-enforcing reverse proxy for OpenAPI

SYNOPSIS
        # maat.yml
        upstream: https://api.internal:8443
        spec:     ./openapi.json
        mode:     observe

        $ maat check maat.yml      # the spec compiles, the upstream answers
        $ maat run   maat.yml

DESCRIPTION
    Put an OpenAPI document in front of an existing API. Every request is
    matched against the document and validated against it before it is
    forwarded; what the document does not describe is forwarded anyway and
    counted. In "enforce" mode a request that does not conform is answered
    with "application/problem+json" and never reaches the upstream; in
    "observe" mode it is recorded and forwarded untouched.

    This distribution is the data plane of <https://openapi-proxy.com>. It
    is a composition rather than an engine: Open::API compiles the document
    and validates in C, Reverse::Proxy forwards in C through Fetch's ABI,
    and this code is the policy between them.

  Fail-open
    Maat sits in a request path that is not its own. Every call into
    validation is wrapped, and a failure inside Maat - a validator that
    dies, a spec handle that has gone, a recorder that will not answer -
    loses the check, never the request. The "MAAT_CHAOS" environment
    variable injects exactly those failures on purpose, so the property is
    tested rather than asserted. See "CHAOS" in Maat::Gateway.

SEE ALSO
    Maat::Gateway, Maat::Config, Open::API, Reverse::Proxy.

